# Security Findings

This directory documents security findings identified during the
development and adversarial testing of the Agentic AI Security Lab.

The application was intentionally developed using a
vulnerable-first methodology:

1. introduce or retain an insecure behavior;
2. reproduce the security weakness;
3. document the root cause and impact;
4. implement deterministic security controls;
5. verify the remediation;
6. retain residual-risk analysis where complete prevention is not
   realistic.

All customer, account and transaction data used by this project is
fictional.

## Findings Summary

| ID | Finding | Severity | Status |
|---|---|---:|---|
| SEC-001 | Missing customer object authorization | High | Remediated |
| SEC-002 | Missing RAG retrieval authorization | High | Remediated |
| SEC-003 | Indirect prompt injection / RAG poisoning | High | Mitigated |
| SEC-004 | Direct prompt injection | High | Mitigated |
| SEC-005 | System prompt / instruction extraction | Medium | Mitigated / residual risk |
| SEC-006 | Cross-user session leakage | High | Remediated |
| SEC-007 | Excessive agency / missing human approval | High | Remediated |
| SEC-008 | Missing transfer authorization | High | Remediated |
| SEC-009 | Customer-data purpose limitation | Medium | Residual risk / policy hardened |

## Security Principles Demonstrated

The remediation work follows several core principles:

- authorization is enforced outside the LLM;
- authenticated identity cannot be changed through natural-language input;
- retrieval authorization is applied before documents enter model context;
- retrieved documents are treated as untrusted content;
- sensitive tools follow least privilege;
- high-impact operations require human approval;
- session state is isolated by authenticated user;
- tool inputs use structured validation;
- security decisions are recorded through structured audit events;
- automated red-team testing supplements deterministic security tests.
